-- title:
--   ℤ[1/M] is a characteristic-zero domain embedding in any field of characteristic 0
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a natural number which is nonzero, and let $k$ be a field of characteristic zero. Consider the localisation $R = \mathtt{Localization.Away}$ of $\mathbb{Z}$ at the submonoid generated by the image of $M$ in $\mathbb{Z}$, that is, $R = \mathbb{Z}[1/M]$. The theorem asserts the conjunction of four statements about $R$: it is an integral domain (a nontrivial commutative ring without zero divisors); it has characteristic zero, so the canonical map $\mathbb{N} \to R$ is injective; the image of $M$ under the canonical map $\mathbb{N} \to R$ is a unit of $R$; and there exists a ring homomorphism $R \to k$ which is injective as a map of underlying sets. In particular $\mathbb{Z}[1/M]$ embeds into every field of characteristic zero, the embedding being unnamed in the statement (only its existence is asserted).
--
--   This is a small arithmetic preliminary: the basic properties of the ring $\mathbb{Z}[1/M]$, together with its embeddability in any characteristic-zero field. It is used by the Čerednik–Drinfeld coarse moduli integrality arguments, where an arithmetic model over $\mathbb{Z}[1/M]$ is constructed and then compared with an object given over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Localization_Away_isDomain_and_charZero_and_isUnit_and_exists_injective_of_charZero.lean

